 \section{Introduction}
 
-Much of the web's commerce is priced by automated auction, and platforms increasingly hand the bidder the forecast. Ad platforms and marketplaces expose a bid landscape or win-rate simulator, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice of bid to the advertiser, seller or buyer, or to an agent acting for them. These choices are made per campaign, keyword or listing rather than per impression, so an agent can afford seconds of reasoning. LLM agents are being built to bid, price and negotiate in such markets \cite{chen2024aucarena,zhu2024synthetic,ahmed2026bazaar}, increasingly on reasoning models that spend tokens before acting. This Web Economics and Digital Society paper asks where the value of reasoning tokens is lost in the bid-landscape decision, and what the wrong bids look like.
+Much of the web's commerce is priced by automated auction, and platforms increasingly hand the bidder the forecast. Ad platforms and marketplaces expose a bid landscape or win-rate simulator, the estimated chance of winning at each candidate bid, and leave the choice of bid to the advertiser, seller or buyer, or to an agent acting for them. These choices are made per campaign, keyword or listing rather than per impression, so an agent can spend seconds on each, but that cost is paid again for every keyword and listing it manages. LLM agents are being built to bid, price and negotiate in such markets \cite{chen2024aucarena,zhu2024synthetic,ahmed2026bazaar}, increasingly on reasoning models that spend tokens before acting. This Web Economics and Digital Society paper asks where the value of reasoning tokens is lost in the bid-landscape decision, and what the wrong bids look like.
 
 The task is deliberately easy. Because the agent receives the estimates, an exact calculator solves it, every lost dollar comes from the agent's reasoning, its answer, or our forcing and read-out protocol, and the calculator prices a perfect tool call. The scope follows from that design: one bidder, a pay-your-bid objective, empirical win curves from eBay closing prices, and $\le$14B open checkpoints, under forced budgets up to 768 tokens and a 1{,}024-token run without forcing. eBay itself uses proxy bidding, under which the errors we find would be harmless; we use its prices only as realistic win curves, score under the pay-your-bid rule of first-price ad exchanges, and report both rules.
 
