 \paragraph{What the wrong bids cost.}
 Whether the pull toward high bids costs money depends on the pricing rule. Under pay-your-bid pricing it does: per 1{,}000 decisions it raises sellers' expected revenue by \$18k--\$83k and lowers bidders' surplus by \$15k--\$36k, mostly on Cartier. Under eBay's own rule, where the winner pays about the closing price, higher bids are weakly better, as theory predicts, and the same choices raise both seller revenue and bidder surplus. The dollar figures in this paper are therefore pay-your-bid figures.
 
